# Onsen packages: what this edition installs beyond stock Quattro, and what
# it takes away. A machine's package set is Quattro's install lists for its
# platform (install/omarchy-base.packages and the rest) plus the lines here,
# minus the "-" lines. onsen update installs what is missing, removes the "-"
# lines, and stops at its drift check on any explicitly installed package
# that neither Quattro's lists nor this file declare.
#
# One package per line. "-name" removes a package. "aur:name" comes from the
# AUR (through yay, as Quattro installs AUR packages). "local:name" is built
# and installed by hand from its own checkout: declared, so it is not drift,
# never installed from here, and named when it is missing. Every other line
# comes from the repositories, so a typo fails instead of reaching the AUR.
# A "# reason" may follow on the same line.
#
# Quattro's lists here mean install/omarchy-base.packages,
# omarchy-x86_64-only.packages, and omarchy-other.packages (the installer's
# own base system and the hardware drivers it picks per machine, including
# the linux-omarchy kernel).

# --- Base system the installer adds outside Quattro's lists ------------------
# The first sync after a fresh install names any other installer package
# missing here.
amd-ucode            # AMD CPU microcode
omarchy-keyring      # omarchy-update-keyring installs it explicitly when missing
efibootmgr           # the installer's UEFI boot entry
mkinitcpio           # the installer's initramfs
sudo                 # the installer's privilege escalation

# --- Removed from stock --------------------------------------------------------
-omarchy-nvim        # the LazyVim starter; config/nvim replaces it
neovim               # was pulled in by omarchy-nvim
# Stock 4.0.4 shipped these; upstream has since dropped them (Signed replaces
# Xournal++, disktree replaces dua), so a machine installed from that ISO
# carries them as leftovers.
